Research code for Faster R-CNN, ODAM, and DPGA-ODAM on COCO-format detection datasets.

Supported training methods:

```bash
python train.py --method baseline ...
python train.py --method odam --odam-weight 0.2 ...
python train.py --method dpga --dpga-warmup 4 --dpga-rampup 4 ...
```

Backbone safety:

- By default the ResNet-50 backbone is randomly initialized and not frozen.
- Use `--backbone-pretrained` when you want torchvision pretrained ResNet-50 weights.
- `--backbone-freeze-at` accepts only `0`, `1`, or `2`.
- Use `--backbone-freeze-at N` only with pretrained weights or a compatible `--init-checkpoint`; the runner verifies the exact frozen-stage keys were loaded and fails closed rather than freezing random layers.

Dataset notes:

- Training annotations must be COCO detection JSON.
- Annotations with `ignore=1` or `iscrowd=1` are encoded as ignore labels.
- RPN anchors overlapping ignore/crowd regions by `--rpn-ignore-overlap` IoA are ignored, not trained as background.
- ROI sampling also excludes ignore/crowd assignments from background fallback.

ODAM/DPGA notes:

- ODAM positive pair masks include self-pairs, matching canonical ODAM behavior.
- DPGA uses the same ODAM loss definition and changes only gradient composition.
- In DPGA runs, `loss_total_objective` is recorded as `NaN` because DPGA does
  not optimize a single scalar `L_det + lambda * L_odam` objective. Use
  `loss_proxy`, `loss_det`, `loss_odam`, `alpha`, `gate`, and gradient
  diagnostics for DPGA reporting.

Progressive ODAM/DPGA ablation:

Use `--experiment-stage` to run one progressive stage at a time. E0-E2 are
ODAM scalarization stages and should be run with `--method odam`; E3-E7 use
DPGA gradient composition and should be run with `--method dpga`.
In stage mode, all stages use the same maximum auxiliary ODAM weight from
`--odam-weight`; `--dpga-alpha` is kept for legacy DPGA runs without
`--experiment-stage`.

| Stage | Warm-up | Hard filtering | Soft reliability | Adaptive score tau | Reliability budget | Projection | Norm-cap | Gate |
|------|------|------|------|------|------|------|------|------|
| E0 | No | No | No | No | No | No | No | No |
| E1 | Yes | No | No | No | No | No | No | No |
| E2 | Yes | Yes | No | No | No | No | No | No |
| E3 | Yes | Yes | No | No | No | Yes | No | No |
| E4 | Yes | Yes | No | No | No | Yes | Yes | No |
| E5 | Yes | Yes | No | No | No | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| E6 | Yes | No | Yes | No | No | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| E7 | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |

Example stage commands:

```bash
python train.py --method odam --experiment-stage E0 ...
python train.py --method odam --experiment-stage E1 ...
python train.py --method odam --experiment-stage E2 ...
python train.py --method dpga --experiment-stage E3 ...
python train.py --method dpga --experiment-stage E4 ...
python train.py --method dpga --experiment-stage E5 ...
python train.py --method dpga --experiment-stage E6 ...
python train.py --method dpga --experiment-stage E7 ...
```

E2-E5 enable ODAM-only hard proposal quality filtering. The default thresholds are
`--odam-min-iou 0.7` and `--odam-min-score 0.9`; filtering affects only
proposals used to construct `loss_rcnn_match`, not detection training targets.
E6 keeps the E5 DPGA controller but disables hard filtering and enables
soft reliability-aware ODAM weighting from proposal-GT IoU and classification
confidence. The default soft transition parameters are
`--odam-reliability-iou-tau 0.6`, `--odam-reliability-iou-temp 0.1`,
`--odam-reliability-score-tau 0.7`, and
`--odam-reliability-score-temp 0.1`.
E7 keeps the E5/E6 DPGA controller, restores light ODAM-only IoU filtering
with `--odam-min-iou 0.5` and `--odam-min-score 0.0`, computes score
reliability from the mini-batch candidate score percentile
`--odam-reliability-score-percentile 0.70`, and applies a per-image/per-GT
reliability budget from 25% to 50% after warm-up with at least two proposals
kept when an object has two or more candidates.

See `experiment_commands.md` for E0-E7 command templates, environment capture,
and the recommended pre-flight smoke checks.

Offline metric reports:

After training has produced run directories such as `outputs/e0` through
`outputs/e7`, use `metrics.py` to aggregate detection, gradient, XAI, and
runtime tables from saved artifacts:

```bash
python metrics.py \
  --outputs outputs \
  --output-dir outputs/metrics_report \
  --val-ann data/coco/valid/_annotations.coco.json \
  --offline-prediction-epoch both
```

This reads `metrics.csv`, `experiment.json`, `predictions_epoch_*.json`,
`gradient_diagnostics_rank*.csv`, `odam_quality_epoch_*.json`, and optional
`xai_quality_best.json` / `xai_quality_last.json` files. It writes
`detection_metrics.csv`, `gradient_metrics.csv`, `xai_metrics.csv`,
`computational_cost_metrics.csv`, `method_metrics_summary.csv`, and
`metric_report.md`.

`offline_*MR-2_Reasonable`, `offline_*MR-2_Heavy`, and `offline_*MR-2_Small`
are recomputed from saved predictions using CityPersons-style filters in the
converted COCO annotations. Use `--citypersons-mr-csv` to merge results from an
external official CityPersons evaluator when preparing paper tables.

XAI metric export:

`Pointing Game` and `Saliency IoU` require ODAM heatmaps and therefore need a
checkpoint forward pass. This is validation-only and does not retrain:

```bash
python export_xai_metrics.py \
  --outputs outputs \
  --runs e0 e1 e2 e3 e4 e5 e6 e7 \
  --checkpoint best \
  --val-images data/coco/valid \
  --val-ann data/coco/valid/_annotations.coco.json \
  --device cuda
```

The export creates `xai_quality_best.json`,
`xai_quality_best_summary.json`, and `xai_quality_best_predictions.json` in
each selected run directory. Run `metrics.py` again after export to populate
`pointing_game` and `saliency_iou` in `xai_metrics.csv`.
